        allocution: "\"I wanted to help.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} had applied for a paid position.", "{DL} carries a lighter for the stoves."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you set those fires?", "\"No. I did not set any of them. I put out fires. I do not start them.\""), .direct("Why were you first on scene so often?", "\"I live close. I hear the call and get there fast. That is all it is.\""), .direct("Had you applied for a paid position?", "\"Yes. I wanted a paid firefighter job. It had nothing to do with the fires.\""), .direct("How long have you volunteered with the department?", "\"Seven years. I started right after high school and have responded to more than a hundred calls. I love the work.\""), .direct("What happens when a call comes in?", "\"My pager goes off, I grab my gear, and I drive straight to the scene. I live about two miles from several of those houses.\""), .direct("Why did you apply for a paid position?", "\"I wanted to make this my career. The department had an opening and I submitted an application and a letter.\""), .cross("The lighter and rags were in your truck, weren't they?", "\"Yes. I carry a lighter for stoves, and rags for cleaning. They are ordinary.\""), .cross("Four of five fires, and you arrived first each time. Doesn't that seem odd?", "\"It looks odd when you put it that way. But I live close to that area.\""), .cross("The same accelerant was used at all five. Where would you get it?", "\"I do not know. I did not use any accelerant. I would never burn a house.\""), .cross("The fires began right before an opening for a paid job. Isn't that motive?", "\"I can see why someone would say that. But I wanted to earn a job by showing up and working, not by creating fires.\""), .cross("Rags and a lighter were in your truck. Why rags?", "\"I use rags for cleaning gear and for wiping my hands. It is an ordinary thing for a firefighter to carry.\"")],
        openings: ("Five vacant houses burned in six weeks, all with the same accelerant. {D}, a volunteer firefighter, was first on scene at four of them, and a lighter and rags were found in {D}'s truck.", "The defense case is that {D} lives close to the fires, is often first on scene, and carries a lighter for stoves like many volunteers."),
        closings: ("Five fires, the same accelerant, a volunteer first on scene four times, and a lighter and rags in the truck. These facts form a pattern.", "A firefighter who lives close and responds fast. No one saw {D} set a fire, and ordinary tools in a truck do not prove arson."),
        timeline: [(-42, "The first fire."), (0, "Week 6: the fifth."), (0, "Week 6: the truck search."), (1, "{DL} is arrested.")],
        identified: "Fire reports.",
        aggravating: ["The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        impact: "The house was my grandmother's.",
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

    static let tentRevivalCures = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.tentRevivalCures",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Traveling Preacher Sold 'Miracle Cure' at Tent Revivals", "Water Sold for $200 a Bottle"],
        law: "falsePretenses100k",
        threshold: 8,
        spec: "Obtained over $100,000 by false pretenses from {V} and others, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D}, a traveling preacher, sold bottled water as a cure for cancer at tent revivals, raising $140,000. Lab tests found tap water. {DL} says it was a blessed gift.",
        state: [E(.forensic, 3, "Lab Report", "Tap water."), E(.document, 3, "Sales Records", "$140,000 in two summers."), E(.statement, 2, "Attendee", "'He said it cured cancer.'")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Blessing", "{DL} says it was a blessed gift.")],
        role: "Attendee",
        exam: [.direct("What did you see at the tent revival?", "\"{D}, a traveling preacher, sold bottled water and told us it could cure cancer. I was there and I bought some.\""), .direct("What did the lab report show?", "\"The water was tap water. The lab found nothing in it other than ordinary tap water.\""), .direct("How much money was raised?", "\"About $140,000 over two summers, according to the sales records. That was a lot for bottled tap water.\""), .direct("What was the revival like?", "\"A large white tent in a field, folding chairs, music, and a preacher who moved through the aisles. People brought sick relatives and lined up for prayer.\""), .direct("What did {D} say about the bottled water?", "\"{D} held up a bottle and said the water was blessed and that those with faith would see sickness leave their bodies. Many people took that to mean cancer.\""), .direct("What did you do?", "\"I bought three bottles for a relative with cancer and paid forty dollars. I gave the bottles to my relative and trusted it.\""), .cross("Did {D} say it would cure cancer in so many words?", "\"The preacher talked about faith and blessing. I understood it to mean a cure, but the words were not always exact.\""), .cross("Were the bottles sold with a price?", "\"There were donations, and some price tags. I cannot say how fixed the amounts were.\""), .cross("Did anyone get hurt?", "\"I know of people who put off treatment. I cannot say all of it was because of the water.\""), .cross("Did {D} ever use the word cure?", "\"I remember the words healing and miracle. I would not swear {D} said cure in so many words.\""), .cross("Did you stop medical treatment because of this?", "\"No. My relative kept going to the doctor. I know some others did stop, but I did not.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Revival volunteer",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Did {D} say the water cured cancer?", "\"I heard {D} say 'faith' and 'blessing.' I never heard a promise of a cure in plain words.\""), .direct("Were prices posted?", "\"Some bottles had price tags and others were by donation. People gave what they could.\""), .direct("What did {D} do with the money?", "\"Some went to the ministry. {D} also gave away many bottles to people who could not pay.\""), .direct("How long have you volunteered with {D}?", "\"Two summers. I set up chairs, ran the sound, and helped at the sales table.\""), .direct("How were the bottles handled at the table?", "\"Some were sold at a posted price and others handed out. If a person could not pay, we gave them a bottle anyway.\""), .direct("What did {D} say about medicine?", "\"{D} told people to follow their doctors and pray too. I heard that more than once.\""), .cross("Did you hear {D} talk about cancer?", "\"Sometimes {D} mentioned sickness. I did not hear a clear cure claim.\""), .cross("Did {D} buy anything big with the money?", "\"I heard about a bus. It was for the ministry, as I understood.\""), .cross("Did anyone ask {D} about the lab results?", "\"Not to my knowledge. I did not hear about the lab until later.\""), .cross("Did {D} ever mention cancer from the stage?", "\"Sometimes in prayer for the sick. I did not hear a promise of a cure.\""), .cross("Were you at every revival?", "\"No. I helped at about half of them. I cannot speak for the ones I missed.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} sold fake cures.", "A blessing."),
        allocution: "\"I believed it.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} bought a bus with the money.", "{DL} gave away many bottles."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you claim the water cured cancer?", "\"I spoke about blessing and faith. I never promised a cure. People heard what they hoped to hear.\""), .direct("What was the price of the water?", "\"It was by donation. I asked people to give what they could, and some gave a lot.\""), .direct("What did you buy with the money?", "\"A bus for the ministry, to carry the revival to more towns. I also gave away many bottles.\""), .direct("How did your ministry begin?", "\"As a small prayer circle. Over the years I started traveling to small towns with a tent and a group of volunteers.\""), .direct("What did you tell crowds about the water?", "\"That it was blessed in prayer and that God could work through it. I invited people to bring their pain to God.\""), .direct("How did you handle money at the revivals?", "\"Volunteers collected donations in baskets and recorded them in a ledger. I paid for fuel, food, and the tent out of it.\""), .cross("The lab says it was tap water, doesn't it?", "\"I know. I called it blessed water. I did not say it came from a spring.\""), .cross("$140,000 in two summers for tap water. Is that fair?", "\"I believed in what I was doing. People gave freely, and I did not push.\""), .cross("People with cancer bought it, didn't they?", "\"Some did. I never told them to skip treatment. I prayed for them.\""), .cross("You asked for money from people with cancer. Didn't you think they hoped for a cure?", "\"I knew they hoped. I told them God decides, and I never said to stop treatment.\""), .cross("Tap water, blessed or not, is not medicine. Did you test it?", "\"No. I did not test it. I did not think of it as medicine, but I can see how the crowd may have.\"")],
        openings: ("A traveling preacher, {D}, sold bottled water at tent revivals and told crowds it could cure cancer, raising $140,000 over two summers. A lab later found only plain tap water in the bottles.", "Weigh the defense proof, which will show that {D} spoke of faith and blessings, never promised a cure in plain words, and took donations that people gave freely and gladly."),
        closings: ("Tap water sold for $140,000 to sick people, with a promise of healing and a bus bought with the proceeds. That is fraud dressed as faith.", "A preacher who spoke of blessings and took freely given donations. The State must prove {D} lied about a cure, and faith is not a fraud."),
        timeline: [(-365, "Revivals begin."), (0, "Summer 2: a death."), (0, "Summer 2: the lab."), (1, "{DL} is charged.")],
        identified: "A lab report.",
        aggravating: ["The victim was particularly vulnerable", "The offense was committed for pecuniary gain"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        impact: "My husband stopped treatment.",
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"],
        genders: ["D": .male])

    static let aideWillChange = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.aideWillChange",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Home Aide Convinced Widower to Rewrite His Will", "Signed Weeks Before His Death"],
        law: "exploitationElder",
        threshold: 8,
        spec: "Exploited an elder adult by deception, obtaining property valued at $210,000, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{V}, an eighty-eight-year-old widower, changed his will on {DATE} to leave his house to {D}, a home aide, two weeks before he died. Doctors said he had dementia. {DL} says {V} wanted to thank {D.him}.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "New Will", "House left to {D}."), E(.document, 3, "Medical Records", "Dementia diagnosis."), E(.statement, 2, "Notary", "'Only {D} was there.'")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Thanks", "{DL} says {V} wanted to thank {D.him}.")],
        role: "Estate attorney",
        exam: [.direct("What did you review about {V}'s will?", "\"{V}, who was eighty-eight, changed the will on {DATE} to leave the house to {D}, a home aide. {V} died two weeks later.\""), .direct("What do the medical records show?", "\"A dementia diagnosis. {V}'s doctors said {V} had dementia at the time of the change.\""), .direct("What did the notary say?", "\"That only {D} was there. No family, no friend, no independent witness. That's unusual for a will.\""), .direct("How did the will come to your attention?", "\"The family hired me after {V} died to review the estate. I found a will signed two weeks before death that gave the house to {D} instead of the relatives.\""), .direct("What did earlier wills provide?", "\"The prior will, signed years earlier, split the house and savings among the family. The change was a sharp break from that plan.\""), .direct("What did the doctors record about {V}'s mental state?", "\"A dementia diagnosis with confusion and memory loss noted at visits both before and after the date the will was signed.\""), .cross("{DL} says {V} wanted to thank {D.him}, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Many people do. But a person with dementia may not understand what that thanks costs.\""), .cross("Did {V} have lucid days?", "\"Possibly. Dementia comes and goes. I can't say how {V} was on that day.\""), .cross("Did {V} leave anything to anyone else?", "\"I'd have to check the prior will. The house went to {D} in the new one.\""), .cross("Is a person with dementia legally barred from changing a will?", "\"No. The question is whether {V} understood the property and the people involved on that day. That is a fact question.\""), .cross("Did {D} take part in drafting the will?", "\"The notary told me {D} was present, but I do not know whether {D} suggested terms.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Neighbor friend",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Do you know {DL} and {V}?", "\"Yes. I lived next door to {V} for years, and I knew {D} from the care visits.\""), .direct("Did {V} mention the plan?", "\"Yes. {V} told me {V.he} wanted to thank {D} for all the care.\""), .direct("How long did you live next to {V}?", "\"Almost twenty years. We shared a fence, a garden, and a lot of meals. I knew {V} well.\""), .direct("What did {V} tell you about {D}?", "\"{V} said {D} was kind and patient and that {V} wanted to leave the house to {D} as thanks. It was in the spring, months before the will.\""), .direct("How did {V} seem when saying that?", "\"Calm and clear. {V} was sitting on the porch and spoke without any pressure from anyone.\""), .direct("What changed about {V}'s mood when {D} came to help each week?", "\"{V} looked forward to it. {V} would wait by the window and tell me {D} was the only one who ever stayed to talk.\""), .cross("When did {V} say that?", "\"Last spring. That was months before the will was changed.\""), .cross("Did {V} have dementia at that time?", "\"I think so, to some degree. {V} had good days, though.\""), .cross("Did you hear anything about the notary visit?", "\"No. I wasn't there.\""), .cross("Did {V} sometimes forget things?", "\"Yes. {V} forgot names and repeated stories. Even so, {V} said the same thing about the house more than once.\""), .cross("Were you with {V} on the day the will was signed?", "\"No. I was not there, so I cannot say what happened at the notary's office.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} took a house.", "A thank-you."),
        allocution: "\"I never asked for it.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} drove {V} to the notary.", "{V} had told a friend about the plan months earlier."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you ask {V} to change the will?", "\"No. {V} brought it up. {V} said {V.he} wanted to thank me for years of care.\""), .direct("Why were you alone with {V} at the notary?", "\"The notary asked others to wait outside. I wasn't pushing anything. I was there to help {V}.\""), .direct("How did you feel about the house?", "\"I was surprised. I never expected it. I cared for {V}, and I'm sorry {V} is gone.\""), .direct("How long did you care for {V}?", "\"About three years. I cooked, bathed {V}, handled medications, and kept {V} company in the evenings.\""), .direct("How did the subject of the house come up?", "\"Months earlier, {V} said how grateful {V.he} was and that {V.he} wanted me to have something. I thought it was talk.\""), .direct("What did you do on the day of the notary visit?", "\"I helped {V} get dressed and went along because {V} asked me to. I waited in the room while {V} signed.\""), .cross("{V} had dementia, didn't {V.he}?", "\"Yes. But {V} knew me and knew what {V.he} wanted. There were good days.\""), .cross("You were the only one with {V} at the notary, weren't you?", "\"Yes. I see how it looks. I didn't pressure anyone.\""), .cross("You inherited a house two weeks before {V} died, didn't you?", "\"Yes. It's a lot. I didn't expect it, and I didn't ask.\""), .cross("You were the only person in the room and you received the house. Do you see how it looks?", "\"I do. But {V} asked me to come. I never raised the subject at the notary's office.\""), .cross("Did you tell any family members about the will before {V} died?", "\"No. I did not know how. I wanted to leave that to {V}, and then it all happened so fast.\"")],
        openings: ("Two weeks before {V}, eighty-eight and living with dementia, died, {V} changed a will to leave the house to {D}, a home aide. The notary says only {D} was present. The State will show the will, the medical records, and the notary.", "The defense will present a longtime neighbor who heard {V} say months earlier that {V.he} wanted to thank {D}. Gratitude is not undue influence."),
        closings: ("A will changed by a person with dementia, a house left to the aide, and a notary who saw only {D}. That's undue influence on a vulnerable person.", "{V} spoke of thanking {D} months before, and a neighbor heard it. The State has not proved {D} pressured {V} into anything. A kind word from a patient is not a crime."),
        timeline: [(-90, "{V} tells a friend."), (0, "The will is signed."), (14, "{V} dies."), (60, "The family contests.")],
        identified: "A will.",
        aggravating: ["The victim was particularly vulnerable", "The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        impact: "He was my father.",
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"],
        genders: ["V": .male])

    static let catfishSingerGiftCards = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.catfishSingerGiftCards",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Man Impersonated Singer Online to Collect Gift Cards", "Fans Sent $18,000"],
        law: "falsePretenses",
        threshold: 7,
        spec: "Obtained property of {V} and others by false pretenses, $18,000, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} posed online as a country singer and collected $18,000 in gift cards from fans on {DATE} for 'fan club fees.' The singer's team had no such club. {DL} says it was a parody account. The account was opened about sixty days before the cards began to arrive. The messages told fans to 'send a gift card to join,' and the singer's team says no such club exists.",
        state: [E(.digital, 3, "Account Records", "The account was opened by {D}."), E(.digital, 3, "Messages", "'Send a gift card to join.'"), E(.document, 2, "Singer's Statement", "No fan club exists.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Parody", "{DL} says it was a parody.")],
        role: "Fan",
        exam: [.direct("How did you come across the account?", "\"I'm a fan of the singer. The account messaged me and told fans to 'send a gift card to join' a fan club.\""), .direct("What did you do?", "\"I sent a gift card because I believed it was real. Many fans did. Altogether, $18,000 in gift cards was collected.\""), .direct("What did you learn later?", "\"The singer's team said no such club exists. The account records show the account was opened by {D} about sixty days before.\""), .direct("What first drew you to the account?", "\"It used the singer's name and photos and posted updates about tours. I followed it for weeks, and the posts felt friendly and personal.\""), .direct("What did the account ask of fans?", "\"A message said to send a gift card to join the fan club and get a signed photo and early tickets. It listed an amount between fifty and two hundred dollars.\""), .direct("How much did you send, and how?", "\"A hundred dollars on a store card. I emailed the code to the address in the message and got a thank-you in return.\""), .cross("{DL} says it was a parody, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Parody doesn't ask fans for gift cards. I sent mine believing it was real.\""), .cross("Did the account say it was a parody?", "\"I didn't see that. If it said so, it wasn't visible to me when I sent the card.\""), .cross("Did you check with the singer's team first?", "\"No. I should have. I got caught up in the excitement.\""), .cross("Did the account use the word parody anywhere you could see?", "\"No. If the word appeared, I never saw it on the main page or in a message.\""), .cross("Did you ever get anything back for your money?", "\"Nothing. I got a couple of replies and then silence. That is when I contacted the singer's team.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Online friend",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Do you know {DL} online?", "\"Yes. We've talked for a while online. I knew about the parody account.\""), .direct("Was it a joke?", "\"{D} called it that. {D} laughed about it with me and said it was for fun.\""), .direct("Did {DL} say anything about money?", "\"Not directly. I didn't think {D} was after money. I took it as a joke.\""), .direct("How do you know {D}?", "\"We met in an online group about music. We chatted most days and joked about silly projects.\""), .direct("What did {D} say about the account?", "\"That it was a bit and that people were reading it as real. {D} laughed and said it was going viral.\""), .direct("Did {D} ever ask you for anything?", "\"No. {D} never asked me for money or cards. {D} treated the account as a game.\""), .cross("Did {DL} tell fans it was a joke?", "\"No. I don't think so. I didn't see any announcement.\""), .cross("Did you see the gift card requests?", "\"I saw some messages. I thought they were part of the bit. I didn't think anyone would pay.\""), .cross("Do you know what happened to the cards?", "\"No. I wasn't involved.\""), .cross("Did you tell fans it was a parody?", "\"No. I saw no reason to. I assumed they could tell.\""), .cross("Did you see {D} receive the cards?", "\"No. I only knew about the account. I did not see what was done with any messages or codes.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} impersonated a singer.", "A parody."),
        allocution: "\"I got carried away.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} redeemed the cards.", "The account said 'parody' in a hidden bio line."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you run a fake fan club?", "\"I ran a parody account. It was a joke. I never meant for people to believe it was real.\""), .direct("Why did you ask for gift cards?", "\"As part of the joke. I thought no one would send real money. I was stunned when they did.\""), .direct("What did you do with the cards?", "\"I held on to them. I didn't want to hurt anyone. I was overwhelmed and didn't know how to stop.\""), .direct("Why did you start the account?", "\"I thought it would be funny to post the way a country singer might. I copied the style and picked a photo.\""), .direct("How did it get out of hand?", "\"Followers started asking about a fan club. I played along as a joke and wrote a message about gift cards, and the replies began.\""), .direct("What did you do when the cards started arriving?", "\"I was shocked. I kept the codes in a note on my phone and told myself I would give them back, and then I did not know how.\""), .cross("The message said to send a gift card to join, didn't it?", "\"Yes. That's what I wrote. It was a joke, but I see how it looked.\""), .cross("$18,000 in gift cards is a lot, isn't it?", "\"It's more than I could believe. I didn't expect any of it.\""), .cross("You opened the account about sixty days earlier, didn't you?", "\"Yes. It grew faster than I expected. I should have stopped sooner.\""), .cross("You sent messages telling fans to send gift cards. A real parody does not collect money, does it?", "\"No. It does not. I let it go too far and I did not stop.\""), .cross("What happened to the $18,000?", "\"I have not spent most of it, but I did use some of the cards. I will not pretend otherwise.\"")],
        openings: ("{D} posed online as a country singer and collected $18,000 in gift cards from fans, telling them to 'send a gift card to join' a fan club that the singer's team says does not exist. The State will show the account records and the messages.", "Jurors will also see a parody account that spun out of control, a friend who heard it was a joke, and no plan to take anyone's money."),
        closings: ("A fake account, a fake fan club, and $18,000 in gift cards from fans who believed. 'Send a gift card to join' is a request for money.", "A parody account grew beyond what {D} expected. The State has not proved {D} meant to deceive fans rather than joke. A joke that went too far is not a scheme to defraud."),
        timeline: [(-60, "The account is made."), (0, "Month 2: cards arrive."), (0, "Month 2: the report."), (1, "{DL} is traced.")],
        identified: "Account records.",
        aggravating: ["The offense was committed for pecuniary gain"],
        mood: .divided,
        impact: "I gave up groceries.",
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"],
        genders: ["D": .male])

    static let payrollTaxSkim = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.payrollTaxSkim",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Payroll Company Owner Kept Clients' Withheld Taxes", "Employers Hit With Penalties"],
        law: "embezzlement100k",
        threshold: 8,
        spec: "Embezzled over $100,000 belonging to {V} and others by a person in a position of trust, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D}, who ran a payroll company, kept $210,000 in tax payments from twelve clients. The IRS billed the clients. {DL} says a software error sent the funds.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "Bank Records", "Tax deposits moved to {D}'s account."), E(.document, 3, "Client Agreements", "{D} handled tax filings."), E(.document, 2, "IRS Notices", "Twelve clients billed.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Software", "{DL} says software sent the funds.")],
        role: "Client business owner",
        exam: [.direct("What did you hire {D}'s company to do?", "\"To handle our payroll and our tax filings. {D} was responsible for sending our payroll taxes to the IRS.\""), .direct("What did the bank records show?", "\"Tax deposits that should have gone to the IRS were moved to {D}'s own account. Twelve clients were affected, for $210,000 total.\""), .direct("What happened to your business?", "\"The IRS billed us for the taxes. We had already paid {D}, and we had to pay again.\""), .direct("How long had you used {D}'s company?", "\"Three years. We sent {D} a payroll file each cycle, and {D} withdrew our payroll taxes and made the IRS payments.\""), .direct("How did you learn there was a problem?", "\"An IRS notice arrived saying a quarter was unpaid. We checked our records and found that our bank showed the payments leaving.\""), .direct("What did {D} say when you called?", "\"{D} said it was a mix-up and would be fixed within a few days. Weeks later, we still had the notice and no explanation.\""), .cross("{D} says a software error sent the funds, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"That is what {D} said. The software vendor logged an error, but I do not know how big it was.\""), .cross("Did {D} notify you of any problem?", "\"No. We learned about it from the IRS. {D} never told us.\""), .cross("Did {D} pay back anything?", "\"I do not know. We have not been repaid, as far as I can tell.\""), .cross("Did you check the IRS account yourself earlier?", "\"No. We trusted {D} to handle it. That was part of what we paid for.\""), .cross("Did any other client's experience match yours?", "\"Yes. Eleven others had the same problem. We compared notices, and each lined up with {D}'s deposit dates.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Software vendor tech",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Was there an error in the payroll software?", "\"Yes, one was logged. It was a routing error in how some deposits were sent.\""), .direct("How big was the error?", "\"Small, as logged. It did not look like it would affect $210,000, but I did not analyze all accounts.\""), .direct("Did {D} report the issue to you?", "\"I do not recall {D} calling about it. I only saw the ticket.\""), .direct("What do you do for the vendor?", "\"I am a support technician. I handle tickets from payroll clients and push fixes when the software misbehaves.\""), .direct("What did the ticket from {D}'s account say?", "\"It reported that some tax deposits had been routed to a wrong account. We logged it and escalated it.\""), .direct("What was the bug?", "\"A mapping error in how a few accounts were stored. It could send a deposit to a fee account if the setup was incorrect.\""), .cross("Could that error send funds to a personal account?", "\"It could redirect deposits, but only if someone set up the account. I do not know about that.\""), .cross("How many clients reported problems?", "\"Twelve that I know of. That is far more than the error would explain.\""), .cross("Did you fix the error?", "\"We patched it. I do not know what happened to the funds.\""), .cross("Could the bug move $210,000?", "\"Not on its own. It would need repeated deposits and an account set up that way.\""), .cross("Did you hear from {D} again after the ticket?", "\"Once, a week later, to ask about a patch. After that, nothing.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} kept clients' taxes.", "A software error."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} bought a lake house.", "A software vendor logged an error."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you keep the clients' tax money?", "\"No. I did not keep it. A software error sent the funds where they should not have gone.\""), .direct("Why was money in your personal account?", "\"The system routed some deposits there. I did not set that up on purpose, and I did not notice at first.\""), .direct("What about the lake house?", "\"I bought it with savings. It had nothing to do with the clients' money.\""), .direct("How did you build the payroll company?", "\"From a small bookkeeping practice into a firm handling a dozen client payrolls. I used a software package to move tax deposits.\""), .direct("What did you find when you looked at the accounts?", "\"Some deposits had been routed to an account that was set up for fees. I thought it was a glitch in the software and opened a ticket.\""), .direct("How did you respond when clients called?", "\"I told them it was being fixed. I should have told them the whole story and told them to call the IRS.\""), .cross("You never told the clients about the error, did you?", "\"No. I should have. I was trying to fix it before telling anyone.\""), .cross("$210,000 moved to your account over time. How is that a small error?", "\"I did not know it was so much. I was behind on reviewing the accounts.\""), .cross("You bought a lake house during the same period, didn't you?", "\"Yes. But it was from my own money. I am not a thief.\""), .cross("You moved $210,000 of other people's tax money into your own account. How could you not notice?", "\"I did not look at that account daily. I was behind and trusting the software.\""), .cross("You never reported it to the IRS yourself, did you?", "\"No. I was trying to fix it quietly. I made a terrible decision.\"")],
        openings: ("{D} ran a payroll company and kept $210,000 in tax payments from twelve clients. The money moved to {D}'s account, the IRS billed the clients, and {D} told no one.", "By the end of this trial, the proof will show that a software error redirected funds and that {D} was slow to notice. A system failure is not a plan to steal."),
        closings: ("Twelve clients paid their taxes to {D}, and {D} kept the money. The IRS sent bills, and the bank records show where it went.", "A logged software error and a small business owner who fell behind. The State must prove {D} took the money on purpose, and a bad system is not a crime."),
        timeline: [(-365, "Deposits move."), (0, "Year 1: IRS notices."), (0, "Year 1: clients complain."), (1, "{DL} is charged.")],
